. The rest of the grid holds groups of single dots, one colour per group, set on a spacing-2 lattice.\n\n**The legend.** Each legend row `(a, b)` means \"the group of colour-`a` dots gets a backing of colour `b`\". The train pairs confirm this:\n- **Pair 1:** legend 5→6, 8→7, 4→3. The 8-dots are backed by 7, the 5-dots by 6, and the 4-dots by 3.\n- **Pair 2:** legend 4→1, 8→7. The 4-dots are backed by 1 and the 8-dots by 7.\n- **Pair 3:** legend 1→3, 8→1, 7→8. The 1-dots are backed by 3, the 8-dots by 1, and the 7-dots (a hollow ring) by 8.\n\nIn the output the legend itself is erased to background.\n\n**The backing shape.** For each dot colour, take the bounding box of all its dots (outside the legend) and grow it by one cell on every side. Fill that whole rectangle with the legend colour, then put the original dots back on top. In pair 1, the 8-dots span rows 4–16 and columns 4–8, and the 7-rectangle fills rows 3–17 and columns 3–9. This also explains pair 3: the 7-ring's rectangle is filled with 8 even inside the ring, apart from where the 1-rectangle sits on top of it.\n\n**Overlaps.** Some rectangles overlap, so we need a stacking order.\n- **Pair 1:** the 6-rectangle covers the 7-rectangle and the 3-rectangle.\n- **Pair 2:** the 1-rectangle covers the 7-rectangle.\n- **Pair 3:** the 1-rectangle covers the 8-rectangle.\n\nIn every case, the legend row listed earlier is on top. So we paint the rectangles from the last legend row up to the first, and then repaint all the dots.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The test legend is 8→4, 5→1, 4→9.\n- The 8-dots (rows 10–16, columns 13–17) get a 4-rectangle spanning rows 9–17 and columns 12–18.\n- The 5-dots (rows 8–12, columns 7–13) get a 1-rectangle spanning rows 7–13 and columns 6–14. It is drawn above the 4-rectangle.\n- The 4-dots (rows 12–18, columns 1–3) get a 9-rectangle spanning rows 11–19 and columns 0–4. Its first column is at the left edge, so the grow-by-one is clipped to the grid border.\n\nFinally the legend corner is cleared and all dots are restored on top.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # read legend: consecutive rows from the top with non-bg in column 0\n    legend = []\n    y = 0\n    while y < H and g[y, 0] != bg and g[y, 1] != bg:\n        legend.append((int(g[y, 0]), int(g[y, 1])))\n        y += 1\n    L = y\n    out = g.copy()\n    out[:L, :2] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Read the legend (dot colour -> backing colour) and erase it.\")\n    field = g.copy()\n    field[:L, :2] = bg\n    # paint backing rectangles from last legend row to first (first ends on top)\n    for a, b in reversed(legend):\n        ys, xs = np.where(field == a)\n        if len(ys) == 0:\n            continue\n        y0, y1 = max(ys.min() - 1, 0), min(ys.max() + 1, H - 1)\n        x0, x1 = max(xs.min() - 1, 0), min(xs.max() + 1, W - 1)\n        out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = b\n        emit(out, f\"Fill the box around the colour-{a} dots (grown by 1) with colour {b}.\")\n    # restore the dots on top\n    mask = field != bg\n    out[mask] = field[mask]\n    emit(out, \"Redraw all original dots on top of the backings.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The legend.** Each legend row `(a, b)` means \"the group of colour-`a` dots gets a backing of colour `b`\"."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1:** legend 5→6, 8→7, 4→3."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 2:** legend 4→1, 8→7."}],"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Finally the legend corner is cleared and all dots are restored on top."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The backing shape.** For each dot colour, take the bounding box of all its dots (outside the legend) and grow it by one cell on every side."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Fill that whole rectangle with the legend colour, then put the original dots back on top."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"the 7-ring's rectangle is filled with 8 even inside the ring, apart from where the 1-rectangle sits on top of it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Overlaps.** Some rectangles overlap, so we need a stacking order."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"3ba9dbb5c6fde58e1f197f53519f28b3f1c1e05af0d59ea84cbf027be59a0891","task_sha256":"7a09df102cf79afd3ccf78c487a247f523389e81bbce2d3e799cea5f37eedcca","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
